WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.822
Global and Remote Teams.

2
00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:06.155
Day 73 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Workplace phase.

3
00:00:07.905 --> 00:00:08.929
The key idea.

4
00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:14.137
When your team spans time zones and cultures, writing well matters more than talking well.

5
00:00:14.417 --> 00:00:20.305
Be explicit, write things down, be clear about time zones, and don't assume others share your context.

6
00:00:21.655 --> 00:00:22.509
Part 1.

7
00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:23.706
Principles.

8
00:00:24.956 --> 00:00:25.959
Write it down:

9
00:00:26.239 --> 00:00:29.225
Decisions, context and next steps go in writing.

10
00:00:29.505 --> 00:00:32.343
Remote teams run on docs, not hallway chats.

11
00:00:32.623 --> 00:00:34.031
Respect time zones:

12
00:00:34.311 --> 00:00:35.825
Always state time zones.

13
00:00:36.105 --> 00:00:39.903
Rotate meeting times so the same people aren't always up late.

14
00:00:40.183 --> 00:00:41.463
Over-communicate:

15
00:00:41.743 --> 00:00:43.300
No one can see you working.

16
00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.396
Post progress, blockers and status changes.

17
00:00:46.676 --> 00:00:48.020
Explicit implicit:

18
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.287
Say Yes, I'll do it by Thursday rather than I'll try.

19
00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:53.465
Avoid idioms and local slang.

20
00:00:54.815 --> 00:00:55.690
Part 2.

21
00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:57.805
Cultural communication styles.

22
00:00:59.055 --> 00:01:00.399
Here is a quick table:

23
00:01:00.679 --> 00:01:03.431
Style, and Some cultures tend to..., and Tips.

24
00:01:03.711 --> 00:01:05.247
Direct versus indirect.

25
00:01:05.527 --> 00:01:07.041
Some cultures tend to...:

26
00:01:07.321 --> 00:01:14.681
US or Netherlands or Germany: direct No, that won't work, India or Japan: softer That may be difficult.

27
00:01:14.961 --> 00:01:15.687
Tips:

28
00:01:15.967 --> 00:01:17.247
Learn to read both.

29
00:01:17.527 --> 00:01:18.615
If unsure, ask:

30
00:01:18.895 --> 00:01:20.644
Just to confirm, is that a no?

31
00:01:20.924 --> 00:01:21.884
Yes means...

32
00:01:22.164 --> 00:01:23.679
Some cultures tend to...:

33
00:01:23.959 --> 00:01:27.543
In India, yes can mean I heard you, not I agree or I can.

34
00:01:27.823 --> 00:01:28.548
Tips:

35
00:01:28.828 --> 00:01:29.937
Say what you mean:

36
00:01:30.217 --> 00:01:34.761
Yes, done by Friday or I can't commit to Friday; Monday is realistic.

37
00:01:35.041 --> 00:01:36.065
Hierarchy.

38
00:01:36.345 --> 00:01:37.860
Some cultures tend to...:

39
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:43.132
Some teams expect juniors to wait to be asked; others expect everyone to challenge.

40
00:01:43.412 --> 00:01:44.137
Tips:

41
00:01:44.417 --> 00:01:50.028
In global teams, speaking up and disagreeing politely is expected, including with seniors.

42
00:01:50.308 --> 00:01:51.311
Small talk.

43
00:01:51.591 --> 00:01:53.105
Some cultures tend to...:

44
00:01:53.385 --> 00:01:58.292
US: short, friendly, Europe: varies, India: personal questions are normal.

45
00:01:58.572 --> 00:01:59.297
Tips:

46
00:01:59.577 --> 00:02:03.545
Avoid salary, age, marriage questions with global colleagues.

47
00:02:04.895 --> 00:02:05.642
Time.

48
00:02:05.922 --> 00:02:07.437
Some cultures tend to...:

49
00:02:07.717 --> 00:02:10.682
Some cultures treat meeting start times as exact.

50
00:02:10.962 --> 00:02:11.687
Tips:

51
00:02:11.967 --> 00:02:14.293
Join on time; 2 minutes late is late.

52
00:02:15.643 --> 00:02:16.283
Tip:

53
00:02:16.563 --> 00:02:17.501
The 'yes' trap.

54
00:02:17.781 --> 00:02:26.955
When a client asks Can you deliver by Friday? and the honest answer is probably not, saying yes to be polite causes a much bigger problem on Friday.

55
00:02:27.235 --> 00:02:29.475
A clear no, but... builds more trust:

56
00:02:29.755 --> 00:02:31.824
Friday is too tight for full testing.

57
00:02:32.104 --> 00:02:33.533
Tuesday is realistic.

58
00:02:33.813 --> 00:02:36.565
Or we ship on Friday without the export feature.

59
00:02:37.915 --> 00:02:38.811
Part 3.

60
00:02:39.091 --> 00:02:40.499
Async communication.

61
00:02:41.749 --> 00:02:42.987
Good async message.

62
00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:45.144
Here is a template you can copy.

63
00:02:45.424 --> 00:02:46.299
Context:

64
00:02:46.579 --> 00:02:53.043
Cart API p95 jumped to 1.2 s after yesterday's deploy (graph attached).

65
00:02:53.323 --> 00:02:59.403
What I found: new N+1 query in getCartItems(), PR 251 fixes it.

66
00:02:59.683 --> 00:03:05.592
Need from you: review PR 251 by your EOD (Thu, 6 pm CET).

67
00:03:05.872 --> 00:03:06.939
If no reply:

68
00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:10.888
I'll merge with Meena's review at 10:00 IST Fri.

69
00:03:13.738 --> 00:03:18.901
Context, then finding, then ask, then deadline (with time zone), then default action.

70
00:03:20.251 --> 00:03:21.915
Handoff across time zones.

71
00:03:22.195 --> 00:03:24.072
Here is a template you can copy.

72
00:03:24.352 --> 00:03:27.253
End-of-day handoff (IST, then US team).

73
00:03:27.533 --> 00:03:30.776
Done: payment retries merged and deployed to staging.

74
00:03:31.056 --> 00:03:35.835
In progress: refund webhook, 70%, branch feat or refund-webhook.

75
00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:39.080
Blocked: need Stripe test keys, @Mike can you share?

76
00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:45.120
Watch out: staging DB restarts at 02:00 UTC (maintenance).

77
00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:49.570
Recording instead of meeting.

78
00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:55.055
For demos and walkthroughs, record a 5-minute Loom-style video plus a written summary.

79
00:03:55.335 --> 00:03:57.767
It saves a meeting at 10 pm for someone.

80
00:03:59.117 --> 00:04:00.035
Part 4.

81
00:04:00.315 --> 00:04:01.957
Meetings across time zones.

82
00:04:03.207 --> 00:04:04.850
Always write times with zone:

83
00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:10.506
Thu 4:30 pm IST or 12 pm CET or 6 am ET.

84
00:04:10.786 --> 00:04:14.242
Prefer the overlap window; rotate inconvenient slots.

85
00:04:14.522 --> 00:04:18.895
Share an agenda and doc beforehand so non-native speakers can prepare.

86
00:04:19.175 --> 00:04:23.570
Summarise in writing afterwards; not everyone catches everything on a call.

87
00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:31.445
Speak slowly and clearly; avoid idioms (ballpark, touch base, low-hanging fruit) with mixed audiences, or explain them.

88
00:04:32.795 --> 00:04:33.755
Part 5.

89
00:04:34.035 --> 00:04:36.104
Accents and understanding each other.

90
00:04:37.354 --> 00:04:38.698
Here is a quick table:

91
00:04:38.978 --> 00:04:40.365
Situation, and Say.

92
00:04:40.645 --> 00:04:41.925
You didn't catch something:

93
00:04:42.205 --> 00:04:44.167
Sorry, the audio cut out for me.

94
00:04:44.447 --> 00:04:46.005
Could you repeat the last part?

95
00:04:46.285 --> 00:04:47.458
Still didn't get it:

96
00:04:47.738 --> 00:04:49.189
Could you type that in the chat?

97
00:04:49.469 --> 00:04:51.282
I want to get it exactly right.

98
00:04:51.562 --> 00:04:52.906
Confirm understanding:

99
00:04:53.186 --> 00:04:56.109
So you need the API by Tuesday your time, correct?

100
00:04:56.389 --> 00:04:57.754
They didn't understand you:

101
00:04:58.034 --> 00:05:01.042
Rephrase with simpler words, don't just repeat louder.

102
00:05:01.322 --> 00:05:02.837
Speaking fast from nerves:

103
00:05:03.117 --> 00:05:07.255
Slow down; pause between points; use the chat for numbers or names.

104
00:05:08.605 --> 00:05:09.544
Part 6.

105
00:05:09.824 --> 00:05:12.555
Common Indian-English phrases in global teams.

106
00:05:13.805 --> 00:05:15.149
Here is a quick table:

107
00:05:15.429 --> 00:05:18.565
Phrase, and Global colleagues may hear, and Use instead.

108
00:05:18.845 --> 00:05:19.847
Do the needful.

109
00:05:20.127 --> 00:05:21.642
Global colleagues may hear:

110
00:05:21.922 --> 00:05:23.095
Unclear request.

111
00:05:23.375 --> 00:05:24.378
Use instead:

112
00:05:24.658 --> 00:05:26.365
Could you please approve the PR?

113
00:05:26.645 --> 00:05:27.711
I have a doubt.

114
00:05:27.991 --> 00:05:29.506
Global colleagues may hear:

115
00:05:29.786 --> 00:05:31.749
I disagree or don't believe it.

116
00:05:32.029 --> 00:05:33.031
Use instead:

117
00:05:33.311 --> 00:05:34.527
I have a question.

118
00:05:34.807 --> 00:05:35.746
Revert back.

119
00:05:36.026 --> 00:05:37.541
Global colleagues may hear:

120
00:05:37.821 --> 00:05:38.930
Undo a change.

121
00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:40.213
Use instead:

122
00:05:40.493 --> 00:05:42.157
Reply / Get back to me.

123
00:05:42.437 --> 00:05:43.290
Prepone.

124
00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:45.085
Global colleagues may hear:

125
00:05:45.365 --> 00:05:46.453
Unknown word.

126
00:05:46.733 --> 00:05:47.735
Use instead:

127
00:05:48.015 --> 00:05:48.997
Move earlier.

128
00:05:49.277 --> 00:05:50.429
Kindly adjust.

129
00:05:50.709 --> 00:05:52.223
Global colleagues may hear:

130
00:05:52.503 --> 00:05:53.421
Confusing.

131
00:05:53.701 --> 00:05:54.703
Use instead:

132
00:05:54.983 --> 00:05:56.050
Could you please...

133
00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:57.375
Out of station.

134
00:05:57.655 --> 00:05:59.170
Global colleagues may hear:

135
00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:00.282
Unclear.

136
00:06:00.562 --> 00:06:01.565
Use instead:

137
00:06:01.845 --> 00:06:03.445
Travelling / Out of town.

138
00:06:03.725 --> 00:06:04.770
Today itself.

139
00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:06.565
Global colleagues may hear:

140
00:06:06.845 --> 00:06:08.103
Unusual phrasing.

141
00:06:08.383 --> 00:06:09.386
Use instead:

142
00:06:09.666 --> 00:06:11.394
Today / By end of today.

143
00:06:12.744 --> 00:06:14.621
See the full list in the Phrase Bank.

144
00:06:15.971 --> 00:06:16.910
Part 7.

145
00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:18.577
Practice conversation:

146
00:06:18.857 --> 00:06:20.606
Confirming across time zones.

147
00:06:21.856 --> 00:06:23.883
Here is how the conversation goes.

148
00:06:24.163 --> 00:06:26.317
Can you get the fix in before our morning?

149
00:06:26.597 --> 00:06:33.808
Just to confirm, your morning is 9 am Eastern time, which is 7:30 pm here in India.

150
00:06:34.088 --> 00:06:35.261
Is that right?

151
00:06:35.541 --> 00:06:36.821
Yes, exactly.

152
00:06:37.101 --> 00:06:42.989
Then yes, I'll have it merged by 6 pm IST, so it's on staging before you start.

153
00:06:44.339 --> 00:06:45.939
The conversation continues.

154
00:06:46.219 --> 00:06:47.158
Great.

155
00:06:47.438 --> 00:06:49.059
And if something goes wrong?

156
00:06:49.339 --> 00:06:56.529
I'll post an update in the channel by 6:30 pm IST either way, with the status and next steps.

157
00:06:56.809 --> 00:06:57.982
Perfect, thanks.

158
00:06:59.332 --> 00:07:00.868
That's the end of this lesson.

159
00:07:01.148 --> 00:07:05.799
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

160
00:07:06.079 --> 00:07:12.201
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

